well i got it completely wrong in 2016  but if you look at the data it seems  clear that  vice president biden is quite a long way  ahead all of the data i can see  points to his victory but  burned by 2016 no one is saying that  they're going to put their head on the  block and  absolutely guarantee it if joe biden  does win  as the polls suggest would that actually  be good news for the united kingdom do  you think he values the special  relationship  i think he values the uk but he's a  serious person  who will follow the power and what he  knows is that when it comes to  the coveted crisis when it comes to the  climate challenge which he's put very  high on the agenda  when he comes to some of the major  global issues he can see that in paris  in berlin and in brussels you've got 27  countries working closely together  and there's no question in my mind that  for the biden camp  brexit has reduced the influence of the  uk  so yes he will be uh warm towards the uk  um but he will also be very realistic  about the kind of issues that we are  still struggling with  and about his urgency for getting things  done  and so i think that puts a special  premium on the british government  not just to get the trade deal with the  european union but also to sort  out the ways in which we are going to  cooperate on security terms on  intelligence terms on foreign policy  terms  with the european union which is  something that really has made  so little progress and that really  leaves us in a weaker position  so it's interesting is that because  biden's course has said that he  would have voted remain he said that  he's regretted the decision do you think  that he  may actually value other european  partners ahead of the uk then  well i think that when it comes to  something like the climate challenge the  european union  is a one of the top three global players  european union america china  are the deciders on the climate change  the european union's  set out a trillion euro plan for  decarbonizing the european  economy that's a major plank of the  biden promise and he wants to see that  as a way of engaging the chinese  as well now britain is hosting the  conference of the parties  to the un convention in november that's  an important element of our diplomacy  but the danger obviously is that we're  there  organizing the meeting but not actually  making the decision  should we really be hoping for a donald  trump victory then i mean he's somebody  who's been much more supportive of  brexit  he's talked about the value of the  special relationship he's also said that  the united kingdom would be at the front  of the queue  for that all-important trade deal  i mean it's interesting because we can  now judge president trump on actions not  just words and  i don't think anyone believes that he's  anything other than  utterly transactional in the way he does  his  business and what we know from the last  year  since the transition from our membership  of the european union started what we  know from the  four years since the referendum uh when  he's been president  is that the eeu us trade deal is going  nowhere for a very simple reason  the needs and the values and the  interests of british consumers  are incompatible with the needs values  and interests of american agriculture  that's just a massive stumbling block  it's the reason why there isn't a eu  us trade deal as well as a uk u.s trade  deal  so i think in the event of a trump  victory the big question for britain  becomes  does he really mean what he says about  neglecting nato does he really mean what  he says about confrontation with china  in every area of international life  because obviously  the current british government has set  out positions on a range of issues that  aren't the same  as the trump uh position and so i think  that if  president trump is re-elected then  democratic countries in europe are going  to find some big questions about how  they engage internationally when the u.s  doesn't want to engage  multilaterally at the same time they're  going back to the all-important  uk u.s trade deal one of the priorities  of course  for any british government working with  the new president at least with donald  trump we've got someone who says that he  wants to make it happen  putting us at the front of the queue  with joe biden all of the same obstacles  would remain  but he's also raised concerns over  things such as the internal markets bill  we know  as you say he's not a fan of brexit  either and he's not  exactly you would say a natural ally of  boris johnson so from that perspective  do you think a trade deal would be more  likely under donald trump  look my experience of this is that  these leaders they in the end work with  the hand that they're dealt  whatever president biden if he becomes  president biden's views of  the british prime minister he's going to  deal with him in a respectful and  serious way so i wouldn't get  too much caught up in the personality  politics i do think there are questions  for the prime minister  about how he shows that he can be a real  partner  talking about those global issues uh  donald trump of course has been  criticized for his foreign policy but at  the same time  he stood up to china he has engaged with  north korea  all of this without starting a war  unlike other previous presidents that  you could think of  should he not be given a bit more credit  i think that the credit for president  trump  is that he's done what he said he would  do he said he had no interest  in international multilateral alliances  and he's shown he meant it by pulling  out of the paris agreement  he said that he was going to make others  pay for things rather than america pay  for it he's followed through on that he  said he doesn't believe in climate  change and he's  shown that by pulling out of the climate  i don't have to agree with those things  but i think that's where if you want to  offer quote-unquote  credit in your word you'd offer that i  think though a sober-minded assessment  would also say  iran is closer to a nuclear weapon today  than it was four years ago  the north korean engagement has not led  to progress  and when it comes to engagement with  china obviously american  farmers and consumers have paid the  price of the tariffs that have  come in and china is actually in a much  stronger position  today than it was four years ago now i'm  keen to  ask you as well about anti-semitism  because of course this is the week  in which the equality and human rights  commission found the labour party had  acted  unlawfully when dealing with complaints  of anti-semitism  how shameful is this for labour  you can't think of anything more  shameful really than to  be found to be colluding conniving in in  what is essentially racist  um behavior or actions and so this is  this is painful this is shameful  as kiyosama said i think he used that  word for every member of the  labour party and it's a stain that has  to be  erased with absolute drive and  determination  and so i think that i reflect on the  fact i never felt  that when i was a member of parliament  for south shields or when i was  foreign secretary no one said oh he's a  jewish member of  parliament he's a jewish foreign  secretary and  what's happened in the labour party  after jeremy corbyn  got elected was that a series of people  came in or felt empowered  to be able to challenge people on the  basis of tropes about their  religion which is an absolute disgrace  and  i think it must be an absolutely central  part of public life to challenge that  and that seems to me what  a kierst armor is now doing but it's  work in progress as jeremy corbyn's  response to the  ehrc report on thursday showed  is it right that jeremy corbyn has been  suspended  yes definitely i mean jeremy corbyn's  response  showed all of the blindness all of the  arrogance all of the sticking of your  fingers in your ears  that we'd seen in his time as leader and  essentially he suspended himself  from the labor party and from the labour  whip in the house of commons i think he  he put himself beyond the pale and it's  essential that he shows that he's  willing to come within the pale  what i suppose i'm struggling to get my  head around is that  jeremy corbyn is now no longer fit to be  a labour mp he's been suspended because  of his reaction  why was it that members of the shadow  cabinet were less than a year ago  campaigning for him to be prime minister  asking people to  give them their vote i mean there's  nothing essentially new  in the report is there do you think the  shadow cabinet need to be taking  or the former shadow cabinet people like  kirsten now need to be taking  a hard look at why they didn't do more  well i think that the report did  actually  say some new things and show some new  things about political  uh interference so i don't think it's  important not to downgrade  the report i also think that the way  gear and angela rainer have been  speaking so plainly  this week about their reflections on the  past  only speaks well for them i mean the  truth about the jeremy corbyn  leadership is that he was never going to  get elected as  a prime minister we fear that he would  get slaughtered in  2017 um but he was just defeated  although  many seats were held by a fraction and  he was slaughtered in  like 2019 with the worst result that  labour has had since 1935 but  he was never going to get elected as a  prime minister but it was essential  to get as many labour mps who are able  to represent their  constituents and so i think this is a  moment when it's time for  the kind of steely determination the  steely leadership that kiyosama has  shown  to be supported and i think that's what  i sense  around things i think i mean i've  obviously not  come from a position of the hard left in  my politics i've always been  more on the center left and i've had  disagreements with those on the hard  left  but never in my worst thoughts that i  think that the hill that they would  choose to fight on  would be the hill of anti-semitism i  mean that's a they put themselves beyond  the pale  and i think the hard questions frankly  are for them at the same time though  um you say that kirstar and angela arena  have been  shown that steely leadership now  speaking out now but they still  campaigned  for jeremy corbyn to be prime minister  at a time when although we may not have  known all the details we did know that  there were issues over the way the  labour party had dealt with  anti-semitism i mean would you have  served in his shadow cabinet  well i'm afraid that there's very little  chance that he would have asked me to  serve in his  government so i can honestly say that is  a question i've never really had to  uh focus uh on i mean the i don't think  he wanted anything to do  with uh people of my political  uh stripe and that's one of the problems  of his of his leadership was the  sectarianism  that he applied to it so i think this  isn't really about  me this is about learning a very simple  lesson and frankly it links to the biden  conversation we're having we're having  if you have a candidate who attracts  people you can win elections and put  your values into practice  if you have a candidate like jeremy  corbyn who repels voters you've got no  chance  of being able to put your values into  practice  and that is the tragedy of british  politics over the last 10 years  obviously i was in a government which  was defeated  10 years ago at the polls and the  tragedy of the last  10 years is that from 2017 2019  british people felt they didn't have a  proper choice and  it must be right and it must be healthy  and it must be good not just for people  like me who are  members of the labour party but for the  country to have an opposition  that is worth its salt and that's what i  hope that pakistan will be able to bring  into practice

